90
00:04:42,813 --> 00:04:46,413
Because your instincts
and mine is, I'm an

91
00:04:46,443 --> 00:04:49,323
automation developer, I
will just write tests.

92
00:04:49,823 --> 00:04:50,623
Here's the thing.

93
00:04:51,223 --> 00:04:54,713
On the left side is the
world we all come from.

94
00:04:55,213 --> 00:04:57,573
You are testing a
deterministic API.

95
00:04:58,033 --> 00:05:02,113
You send a payload, you get
back two hundred, you assert

96
00:05:02,133 --> 00:05:04,583
actual equals expected.

97
00:05:05,073 --> 00:05:06,363
It's a peaceful contract.

98
00:05:06,363 --> 00:05:09,123
Input in, output
asserted, done.

99
00:05:09,453 --> 00:05:10,513
You can sleep at night.

100
00:05:11,083 --> 00:05:13,203
Now, look at the right side.

101
00:05:14,053 --> 00:05:16,513
Testing an AI agent
is not a contract.

102
00:05:16,794 --> 00:05:18,313
It's a total guessing game.

103
00:05:19,173 --> 00:05:23,244
You send a prompt, and what
comes back is question marks.

104
00:05:23,644 --> 00:05:27,083
Natural language goes in,
and through a chain of

105
00:05:27,123 --> 00:05:31,084
tool calls, executable
code comes out, and it's

106
00:05:31,094 --> 00:05:32,684
non-deterministic by design.

107
00:05:33,263 --> 00:05:36,494
Run it twice, get two
different answer, both

108
00:05:36,813 --> 00:05:37,784
potentially valid.

109
00:05:38,354 --> 00:05:41,304
So what's my assertion
supposed to be?

110
00:05:41,953 --> 00:05:45,764
Expect AI to not
invent fake APIs?

111
00:05:46,264 --> 00:05:48,754
Here's the
uncomfortable truth.

112
00:05:49,323 --> 00:05:53,204
If you're validating AI
output with regex and few

113
00:05:53,213 --> 00:05:56,384
unit tests, you are not
testing, you're hoping.

114
00:05:56,684 --> 00:05:59,533
And hope is not
a test strategy

115
00:06:00,033 --> 00:06:04,333
Okay, Liran, you are thinking
there is a whole field of

116
00:06:04,413 --> 00:06:09,573
NLP metrics for scoring
text, BLEU, ROUGE, use those.

117
00:06:10,333 --> 00:06:14,963
And you should could have in
two thousand and eighteen.

118
00:06:15,473 --> 00:06:17,303
These two used to
be the standard.

119
00:06:17,553 --> 00:06:18,403
Not anymore.

120
00:06:19,383 --> 00:06:22,293
Here's why they don't
wro-- for-- work for us.

121
00:06:22,793 --> 00:06:27,083
Those metrics only measure
one thing, word overlap.

122
00:06:27,523 --> 00:06:30,633
Literally, how many words
in your output match the

123
00:06:30,644 --> 00:06:32,024
reference word for word.

124
00:06:32,513 --> 00:06:35,073
So watch what
happens with code.

125
00:06:35,573 --> 00:06:39,353
Your AI writes a function
that is completely correct,

126
00:06:39,453 --> 00:06:43,393
but it's named a variable
result instead of output.

127
00:06:43,893 --> 00:06:48,293
Or it's used a for loop where
your reference used a while.

128
00:06:49,033 --> 00:06:50,823
Functionally identical.

129
00:06:51,094 --> 00:06:54,773
Score, fail, because the
words didn't line up.

130
00:06:55,393 --> 00:07:00,144
Meaning, reasoning, intent,
everything we actually care

131
00:07:00,144 --> 00:07:03,813
about in generated code
is completely invisible

132
00:07:03,913 --> 00:07:05,483
to word overlap scoring.

133
00:07:06,053 --> 00:07:08,803
In fact, there is a
lovely finding from

134
00:07:08,813 --> 00:07:09,993
Hendrix and his team.

135
00:07:10,563 --> 00:07:14,713
For code, this kind of score
can actually go up while

136
00:07:14,724 --> 00:07:16,654
your code get more broken.

137
00:07:17,514 --> 00:07:18,524
Let that sink in.

138
00:07:19,024 --> 00:07:22,364
So we don't need a
metric that counts word.

139
00:07:23,124 --> 00:07:26,044
We need something that
understand context.

140
00:07:26,234 --> 00:07:29,464
And in two thousand
twenty-six, we happen to have

141
00:07:29,464 --> 00:07:31,434
exactly that laying around

142
00:07:31,934 --> 00:07:34,744
So here's the idea
that change everything.

143
00:07:35,524 --> 00:07:39,784
If understanding context is
exactly what large language

144
00:07:39,794 --> 00:07:44,044
model are good at, why not
using one strong LLM to

145
00:07:44,044 --> 00:07:45,564
grade the output of another?

146
00:07:46,444 --> 00:07:48,184
That's LLM as a judge.

147
00:07:48,934 --> 00:07:53,344
You take a capable model,
you hand it a rubric

148
00:07:53,364 --> 00:07:56,094
that you defined, and
you ask it to score.

149
00:07:56,534 --> 00:07:59,974
It's basically a senior
developer reviewing

150
00:08:00,024 --> 00:08:00,874
a pull request.

151
00:08:01,444 --> 00:08:05,414
Expect this one is available
at three AM, never get tired,

152
00:08:05,424 --> 00:08:07,004
and never needs a coffee.

153
00:08:07,683 --> 00:08:12,174
And I know the skeptical face
some of you are making right

154
00:08:12,204 --> 00:08:13,774
now because I made it too.

155
00:08:14,384 --> 00:08:18,923
"Wait, so we are using
AI to check AI?" Fair.

156
00:08:19,483 --> 00:08:22,813
So let's look at whether
it's actually worth.

157
00:08:23,774 --> 00:08:26,524
The fundamental research
here is Empty Bench

158
00:08:26,524 --> 00:08:27,743
and Chatbot Arena.

159
00:08:28,193 --> 00:08:32,803
Zeng and colleague, they
measure how often GPT-4 judge

160
00:08:32,804 --> 00:08:37,293
