on the clear-cut cases.

161
00:08:37,733 --> 00:08:40,854
The answer, around
eighty-five percent.

162
00:08:41,053 --> 00:08:43,814
Now, eighty-five
percent, is that good?

163
00:08:44,453 --> 00:08:45,204
Here's the kicker.

164
00:08:45,733 --> 00:08:49,043
Humans only agree with
each other about eighty-one

165
00:08:49,044 --> 00:08:50,463
percent of the time.

166
00:08:50,753 --> 00:08:54,183
So the AI agent isn't
just close to humans.

167
00:08:54,553 --> 00:08:58,323
On this benchmark, it's
past the point where humans

168
00:08:58,364 --> 00:08:59,894
even agree with themselves.

169
00:09:00,673 --> 00:09:04,344
That's when I stop being a
skeptic and start building.

170
00:09:04,844 --> 00:09:09,194
So how do you actually
build one of these without

171
00:09:09,744 --> 00:09:10,974
it being a black box?

172
00:09:11,974 --> 00:09:13,654
Let's look under,
under the hood.

173
00:09:14,424 --> 00:09:16,394
There are three
question you answer

174
00:09:16,394 --> 00:09:17,944
when you design a judge.

175
00:09:18,694 --> 00:09:19,454
What to judge?

176
00:09:20,044 --> 00:09:23,384
Correctness, meaning,
compliance, safety.

177
00:09:24,204 --> 00:09:24,944
How to judge?

178
00:09:25,414 --> 00:09:28,124
Do you score one
output against a golden

179
00:09:28,124 --> 00:09:31,504
reference or compare two
outputs head-to-head?

180
00:09:32,074 --> 00:09:36,034
And where you're judging
a single answer or a whole

181
00:09:36,034 --> 00:09:39,663
multi-turn agent run, every
tool call in the chain.

182
00:09:40,434 --> 00:09:44,093
Now, the part everyone
gets wrong, making

183
00:09:44,094 --> 00:09:45,284
the judge reliable.

184
00:09:45,754 --> 00:09:48,973
Because a basic judge
can be unreliable, so

185
00:09:49,174 --> 00:09:50,464
we have four principle.

186
00:09:51,174 --> 00:09:53,984
One, GEval, plus
a decision tree.

187
00:09:54,533 --> 00:09:57,573
You let the judge reason
through its own ste-steps,

188
00:09:58,114 --> 00:10:01,574
then turns this into
series of yes, no calls.

189
00:10:02,254 --> 00:10:05,614
Techniques called
DAG and Quad, so it

190
00:10:05,643 --> 00:10:06,764
can't handle a wave.

191
00:10:07,474 --> 00:10:09,814
Two, and please
write this down.

192
00:10:10,593 --> 00:10:13,754
Don't ask for a score
from one to ten.

193
00:10:14,294 --> 00:10:16,883
LLMs are not good
with exact number.

194
00:10:17,383 --> 00:10:22,284
Ask for a small set of labels
like pass, partial, fail.

195
00:10:23,093 --> 00:10:26,414
Three, enter it with
a golden reference and

196
00:10:26,414 --> 00:10:27,564
couple of examples.

197
00:10:28,133 --> 00:10:30,914
For code, one good
example often beats ten.

198
00:10:31,354 --> 00:10:36,164
And four, never let a model
grade on his own homework.

199
00:10:36,664 --> 00:10:40,563
If GPT wrote the
code, Claude judge it.

200
00:10:41,163 --> 00:10:42,144
Different family.

201
00:10:42,644 --> 00:10:45,784
Get these four, four right
and your shaky intern

202
00:10:45,793 --> 00:10:47,853
becomes a reliable reviewer.

203
00:10:48,353 --> 00:10:50,993
Now, I promise you honesty.

204
00:10:51,653 --> 00:10:54,903
So let's talk about where
our judge is a little

205
00:10:54,903 --> 00:10:56,463
bit dumb, because it is.

206
00:10:56,963 --> 00:10:59,553
Four blind spots you
have to know about.

207
00:11:00,223 --> 00:11:01,263
Verbosity bias.

208
00:11:01,573 --> 00:11:03,233
Judge loves a long answer.

209
00:11:04,193 --> 00:11:06,873
Give them two correct
solution, and they will

210
00:11:07,083 --> 00:11:10,683
reward the wordy one over
the clean one every time.

211
00:11:11,613 --> 00:11:13,503
Position bi- bias.

212
00:11:13,533 --> 00:11:18,343
In a head-to-head, they
tend to favor whichever

213
00:11:18,433 --> 00:11:19,833
option you showed first.

214
00:11:20,333 --> 00:11:21,943
Self-enhancement bias.

215
00:11:22,333 --> 00:11:25,713
Model quietly prefer
output that looks like

216
00:11:25,713 --> 00:11:26,943
their own family style.

217
00:11:27,543 --> 00:11:29,283
And plain non-determinism.

218
00:11:29,583 --> 00:11:33,583
Same input, slightly different
score and on a different run.

219
00:11:34,303 --> 00:11:35,863
Sound like a deal
breaker, right?

220
00:11:36,453 --> 00:11:39,403
It's not, because each
of those has a fix.

221
00:11:39,903 --> 00:11:45,743
For position bias, you swap
A to B and run it again.

222
00:11:46,533 --> 00:11:49,043
If the winner changed,
you have got it.

223
00:11:49,773 --> 00:11:51,673
And here's my favorite trick.

224
00:11:52,403 --> 00:11:55,673
That run-to-run wobble
isn't noise, it's a map.

225
00:11:56,493 --> 00:11:58,833
The judge only get
shaky on the really

226
00:11:58,833 --> 00:12:00,613
tricky, unclear cases.

227
00:12:01,033 --> 00:12:04,313
So the places where
it's disagrees with

228
00:12:04,343 --> 00:12:08,043
itself are exactly the
places your instruction

229
00:12:08,343 --> 00:12:09,573
weren't clear enough.

230
00:12:10,083 --> 00:12:14,353
And one more, models
drift under your feet.

231
00:12:15,103 --> 00:12:17,523
Same model, different
behavior next month.

232
00:12:17,693 --> 00:12:22,833
So log every score ev-- over
time and alert when it slips.

233
00:12:23,333 --> 00:12:25,653
The judge's wickedness,
if you listen to them,

234
00:12:26,123 --> 00:12:27,533
tell you where to improve

235
00:12:28,033 --> 00:12:32,333
Which bring us to the
question you have all

236
00:12:32,363 --> 00:12:33,763
been waiting to ask me.

237
00:12:34,273 --> 00:12:35,093
Finally, Run.

238
00:12:35,443 --> 00:12:39,323
The judge checks the agent,
but who checks the judge?

239
00:12:40,153 --> 00:12:41,033
Great question.

240
00:12:41,383 --> 00:12:45,323
The answer is a process
called meta-evaluation, and

241
00:12:45,323 --> 00:12:47,183
it's simpler than it sounds.

242
00:12:47,703 --> 00:12:50,153
Step one, you build
a small golden set.

243
00:12:50,743 --> 00:12:54,203
A few thousand example
that a human has already

244
00:12:54,203 --> 00:12:55,103
labeled correctly.

245
00:12:55,573 --> 00:12:56,273
Ground truth.

246
00:12:56,913 --> 00:13:01,703
Step two, you run your or
your judge over that same set.

247
00:13:02,653 --> 00:13:05,143
Step three, you
measure the agreement.

248
00:13:05,823 --> 00:13:08,113
How often did judge
match the human?

249
00:13:08,623 --> 00:13:11,683
That number is your
human alignment, and

250
00:13:11,683 --> 00:13:14,443
it's the single most
important number you have.

251
00:13:15,253 --> 00:13:17,443
Step four, you
read the misses.

252
00:13:18,163 --> 00:13:21,023
Every place that judge
disagreed with the

253
00:13:21,023 --> 00:13:22,983
human, you go look.

254
00:13:23,153 --> 00:13:26,833
And usually, it's not
that the judge is stupid,

255
00:13:27,363 --> 00:13:30,143
it's that your instruction
were a bit fuzzy.

256
00:13:30,693 --> 00:13:33,743
So you tighten
them and go again.

257
00:13:34,223 --> 00:13:34,883
It's a loop.

258
00:13:35,243 --> 00:13:38,963
Trust, but verify, and
then verify the checker.

259
00:13:39,453 --> 00:13:43,163
And yeah, someone always
asks, "But who checks the

260
00:13:43,173 --> 00:13:47,443
checker?" A little bit true,
but here's why it's hard.

261
00:13:47,943 --> 00:13:51,633
The judge has one error
job and an answer key.

262
00:13:52,333 --> 00:13:54,773
That's much easier
problem than writing

263
00:13:54,813 --> 00:13:56,273
code from scratch.

264
00:13:56,863 --> 00:14:00,723
It's a lot easier to check
an answer than to come up

265
00:14:00,723 --> 00:14:04,933
with… The gap is what makes
the whole thing stand up

266
00:14:05,433 --> 00:14:07,353
Okay, theory is over.

267
00:14:07,823 --> 00:14:11,753
Let me show you the POC
I built to solve this.

268
00:14:12,523 --> 00:14:16,073
No single tool catches
everything, so I stacked

269
00:14:16,073 --> 00:14:20,783
three, and each one catch what
the other completely missed.

270
00:14:21,283 --> 00:14:23,043
Layer one, deterministic.

271
00:14:23,383 --> 00:14:24,473
The reality check.

272
00:14:24,763 --> 00:14:25,963
Does it even compile?

273
00:14:26,583 --> 00:14:29,133
Layer two, our LLM as a judge.

274
00:14:29,443 --> 00:14:30,993
Does it mean the right thing?

275
00:14:31,863 --> 00:14:33,603
Layer three, red team.

276
00:14:33,893 --> 00:14:34,823
Can it be tricked?

277
00:14:35,673 --> 00:14:40,283
Syntax, meaning, and
security, and the important

278
00:14:40,313 --> 00:14:44,023
part, this whole stack
runs automatically on every

279
00:14:44,023 --> 00:14:48,223
pull request that touch the
agent's prompt or its tools.

280
00:14:48,643 --> 00:14:50,913
No human is to
remember to run it.

281
00:14:51,413 --> 00:14:53,873
Let's take them one at a time.

282
00:14:54,263 --> 00:14:57,273
They are each kind of
beautiful in how little

283
00:14:57,303 --> 00:14:58,143
they trust the AI

284
00:14:58,643 --> 00:15:00,943
Layer one, the reality check.

285
00:15:01,373 --> 00:15:04,563
And this one is really
simple in the best way.

286
00:15:05,443 --> 00:15:06,143
Here's the trap.

287
00:15:06,493 --> 00:15:09,453
An AI gives you code in
a nice markdown block

288
00:15:09,453 --> 00:15:12,413
with syntax highlighting,
and it's look perfect.

289
00:15:13,273 --> 00:15:16,243
Looking perfect is not
the same as working.

290
00:15:16,583 --> 00:15:20,953
So layer one strips away all
the pretty formatting, takes

291
00:15:20,963 --> 00:15:24,913
the raw output, and does the
most boring possible thing.

292
00:15:25,333 --> 00:15:27,313
It actually tries
to compile it.

293
00:15:27,813 --> 00:15:32,093
From an API call, that's
mean checking it against

294
00:15:32,113 --> 00:15:33,403
the real API spec.

295
00:15:33,923 --> 00:15:37,013
For generated code,
it means parsing it.

296
00:15:37,423 --> 00:15:38,703
Does it even compile?

297
00:15:39,453 --> 00:15:42,573
And it catches the stuff
that should never reach a

298
00:15:42,573 --> 00:15:46,453
human reviewer Hallucinated
endpoints, calls to routes

299
00:15:46,483 --> 00:15:48,523
that simply doesn't exist.

300
00:15:49,233 --> 00:15:52,393
Broken JSON the real
API would reject.

301
00:15:52,943 --> 00:15:54,693
Code that doesn't even parse.

302
00:15:55,403 --> 00:15:57,743
The rule is brutal and simple.

303
00:15:58,203 --> 00:16:03,573
If it doesn't compile or the
format is wrong, the pull

304
00:16:03,573 --> 00:16:05,553
request fails instantly.

305
00:16:06,083 --> 00:16:08,793
No opinion, no
LLM, no discussion.

306
00:16:09,483 --> 00:16:13,143
But, and this is the
catch, code can compile

307
00:16:13,183 --> 00:16:17,233
perfectly and still do
completely the wrong thing.

308
00:16:18,013 --> 00:16:20,613
Passing the syntax
check doesn't means it's

309
00:16:20,613 --> 00:16:23,823
correct, and that exactly
why we need Layer 2

310
00:16:24,323 --> 00:16:29,003
So layer two, this is
where our robot courtroom

311
00:16:29,583 --> 00:16:31,273
finally comes into session.

312
00:16:31,773 --> 00:16:34,563
Picture code that
compiles flawlessly.

313
00:16:35,313 --> 00:16:39,583
Layer one is thrilled, but
the logic is quietly wrong.

314
00:16:40,253 --> 00:16:44,063
You ask it to block builds
with critical vulnerabilities,

315
00:16:44,063 --> 00:16:47,823
and instead is quietly
fetch a list of user.

316
00:16:48,713 --> 00:16:51,153
Valid code, completely
wrong meaning.

317
00:16:51,653 --> 00:16:53,123
Layer one has no idea.

318
00:16:53,603 --> 00:16:57,113
This layer entire job
is to grade meaning.

319
00:16:57,803 --> 00:16:59,913
So how does it flow?

320
00:17:00,843 --> 00:17:03,493
We keep a golden
dat-dataset, thirty plus

321
00:17:04,033 --> 00:17:07,833
curated pairs of here's the
request in plain English,

322
00:17:08,233 --> 00:17:09,613
here's the correct code.

323
00:17:10,223 --> 00:17:14,563
The prompt goes to the agent
generates code, and then the

324
00:17:14,573 --> 00:17:18,703
judge LLM scores that code
against our golden reference.

325
00:17:19,293 --> 00:17:22,533
And remember what
I said earlier, it

326
00:17:22,573 --> 00:17:23,773
doesn't guess a number.

327
00:17:24,293 --> 00:17:28,683
It's turn a few simple checks,
pass or fail, and those add

328
00:17:28,713 --> 00:17:33,113
up to meaning score between
zero and one One clean number

329
00:17:33,583 --> 00:17:35,763
built from clear yes/no calls.

330
00:17:36,263 --> 00:17:38,673
Two tools makes
this real for us.

331
00:17:39,163 --> 00:17:42,383
Promptthrough runs
the whole evaluation.

332
00:17:42,763 --> 00:17:45,823
Think of it as a test
runner for prompts.

333
00:17:46,703 --> 00:17:51,193
And Helicon gives us tracing,
so when generation fail, I

334
00:17:51,193 --> 00:17:55,583
can see exactly why instead
of just starting at a Red

335
00:17:55,583 --> 00:17:59,683
X. And the rubric, the
eval rubric, is where your

336
00:17:59,713 --> 00:18:00,753
domain knowledge lives.

337
00:18:01,343 --> 00:18:04,333
It's the thing that tells
the judge what correct

338
00:18:04,333 --> 00:18:07,373
